    fix(mcp): assert constructor-escape probe outcome and document trust boundary (MCP-CUSTOM-RUNTIME-002)
    
    - custom-runtime.ts (LIVE escape, fixed): the JS sandbox injected HOST-realm
      intrinsics (args, Object, JSON, WebAssembly, Error, ...). Every injected host
      object carries `.constructor.constructor === <host Function>`, and host code
      generation is not gated by this context's codeGeneration flag, so
      `this.constructor.constructor('return process')()` returned the real host
      `process` — a full sandbox escape from a "sandboxed" tool. The context object
      is now prototype-less (Object.create(null)) and carries one primitive string
      (the args JSON); a prelude parses the args INSIDE the realm, so `args` is a
      realm object, not a host object. codeGeneration.strings:false (previously
      defeated by the injected constructors) now makes the realm's Function/eval
      throw "Code generation from strings disallowed for this context", and
      codeGeneration.wasm:true keeps the realm's OWN WebAssembly usable for
      self-contained compute — wasm modules still instantiate with an EMPTY import
      namespace, so no host function can be linked.
    - custom-runtime.ts (documentation): top-of-file TRUST BOUNDARY block stating
      what IS protected — wall clock (vm timeout + worker.terminate), V8 heap
      ceiling, host capability denial, empty wasm imports, fail-closed capability
      declarations, result hygiene — and what is NOT: no OS-level isolation (same
      process/uid/filesystem/network namespace; node:vm is documented by Node as
      not a security mechanism), so the threat model is operator-authored config
      code, and real isolation must run out of process.
    - custom-runtime.test.ts: the constructor-escape probe no longer computes
      `typeof this.constructor.constructor` and discards it. It now asserts the
      surface is reachable (function) — so the probe cannot pass vacuously — and
      asserts 10 escape arms (this / globalThis / args / Object / [] / '' /
      console.log / Function / eval / require-via-constructor) each come back
      blocked with the code-generation message, plus that `args` is a realm object.
      New tests pin that in-realm WebAssembly still computes (add(2,3)=5) and that
      a wasm module with an env import still cannot link inside the JS sandbox.
    - RED proof (pre-fix revision 338027ad, captured copy still holds no
      Object.create(null)): the new probe FAILS with "thisCtorEscape must be
      blocked: expected 'NOT-BLOCKED:object' to match /^blocked:/" — the escape
      yielded host `process`. Restored fix: green.
    - Verified: pnpm exec vitest run src/mcp/custom-runtime.test.ts (26 passed),
      pnpm exec tsc --noEmit (clean, exit 0).
    
